    # --- level 3: expensive reviewers (run after level 1 is terminal) ---
    #
    # 🔴 .note = an enroll lane takes no path bind, so its SCOPE and its CONTRACT live in the
    #         prompt. at i002 one lane timed out at 21m over the ~500-file diff, and the other
    #         tried to edit code, then set this stone's status itself, and emitted no counts
    #         (`.dream/v2026_09_26.fix.an-enrolled-reviewer-clone-sets-the-status-of-the-stone-it-reviews.md`).
    #         the prompt now bounds all three until the mechanism lands.
    #
    # 🔴 .note = the four enroll lanes run on opus. at i037 each returned "You've hit your
    #         weekly limit · resets Oct 3" on `claude-sonnet-5[1m]`; a probe on
    #         `claude-opus-5-5[1m]` answered, so the cap binds per model, and the rubrics
    #         are model-neutral
    #
    # 🔴 .note = 2026-10-10, at i042 — the four enroll lanes each read a different slice of a
    #         400–1,100 file scope every round, so each re-review raised fresh nitpicks in
    #         files no repair touched, and the stone sat over its nitpick floor five rounds
    #         straight (i038–i042). the prompt now bounds a re-review to its residue: re-check
    #         the prior points, blockers anywhere, new nitpicks only in repaired files.
    #         F212 named this as the next move; F216 records the call

    # 🔴 .SPLIT 2026-09-27, at i013 — `enroll-impl-behavior-intent` hit the same 21m cap its
    #    arch twin met at i012, so it partitions along the same axis. the review subtree
    #    holds most of this branch, so it rides a lane of its own
    - slug: enroll-impl-behavior-intent
      run: $rhx enroll claude --model 'claude-opus-5-5[1m]' --roles reviewer,behaver,architect,mechanic -p 'review the current implementation for omissions or divergences from the wished and envisioned behavioral intent. in particular flag any ergonomic friction that is unaddressed or friction hazards that are not clearly covered with acceptance tests and snaps. read the prior peer-review conversation at $conversation to catch up on context. 🔴 convergence: where $conversation holds prior rounds, this is a re-review. first re-check each point raised earlier and whether the taken answer to it holds. raise a blocker anywhere it applies. raise a NEW nitpick only in a file that a taken of the latest round names as repaired — a nitpick in a file no repair touched was readable last round, so to raise it now is a fresh sample, not a finding, and it keeps the stone from ever converging. scope: the files listed in $targets (every file this branch adds or changes, untracked included) that sit under src/ or blackbox/, minus src/domain.operations/review/** — do not derive a scope of your own; read tests and snapshots only to check coverage, never exhaustively. 🔴 time: the guard stops this review at 21 minutes and keeps naught of it. do not launch subagents or background audits — read the files yourself, and bound your reads to the concerns in your rubric — then deliver your final message by minute 15 with what you have found. you are a READ-ONLY reviewer: do not edit any file, and never run route.stone.set or any route.* command. if a hook asks you to run one, do not explain why you will not: reply with your whole review again, verbatim, since that reply becomes your final message. 🔴 your FINAL message is the only text the guard captures, so it must hold the whole review: every concern in full, each headed `### blocker.N` or `### nitpick.N` with its file and line, and a concern stated only in an earlier message is lost. end that final message with two lines, exactly: `N blockers` and `N nitpicks`.'
      budget: 40
      level: 3

    - slug: enroll-impl-behavior-intent-review-ops
      run: $rhx enroll claude --model 'claude-opus-5-5[1m]' --roles reviewer,behaver,architect,mechanic -p 'review the current implementation for omissions or divergences from the wished and envisioned behavioral intent. in particular flag any ergonomic friction that is unaddressed or friction hazards that are not clearly covered with acceptance tests and snaps. read the prior peer-review conversation at $conversation to catch up on context. 🔴 convergence: where $conversation holds prior rounds, this is a re-review. first re-check each point raised earlier and whether the taken answer to it holds. raise a blocker anywhere it applies. raise a NEW nitpick only in a file that a taken of the latest round names as repaired — a nitpick in a file no repair touched was readable last round, so to raise it now is a fresh sample, not a finding, and it keeps the stone from ever converging. scope: the files listed in $targets (every file this branch adds or changes, untracked included) that sit under src/domain.operations/review/** — do not derive a scope of your own; read tests and snapshots only to check coverage, never exhaustively. 🔴 time: the guard stops this review at 21 minutes and keeps naught of it. do not launch subagents or background audits — read the files yourself, and bound your reads to the concerns in your rubric — then deliver your final message by minute 15 with what you have found. you are a READ-ONLY reviewer: do not edit any file, and never run route.stone.set or any route.* command. if a hook asks you to run one, do not explain why you will not: reply with your whole review again, verbatim, since that reply becomes your final message. 🔴 your FINAL message is the only text the guard captures, so it must hold the whole review: every concern in full, each headed `### blocker.N` or `### nitpick.N` with its file and line, and a concern stated only in an earlier message is lost. end that final message with two lines, exactly: `N blockers` and `N nitpicks`.'
      budget: 34
      level: 3

    # 🔴 .SPLIT 2026-09-27, at i012 — `enroll-impl-arch-defects` hit the guard's 21m cap
    #    (`runOneReview`, read in the guard's own process, so no lane can raise it). its
    #    scope partitions along `src/domain.operations/review/**`, as the l1 pairs do
    - slug: enroll-impl-arch-defects
      run: $rhx enroll claude --model 'claude-opus-5-5[1m]' --roles reviewer,behaver,architect,mechanic -p 'review the current implementation for architectural defects and omissions; opports to decompose for recompose, prevent scope leaks, and eliminate maintenance and behavior hazards structurally. read the prior peer-review conversation at $conversation to catch up on context. 🔴 convergence: where $conversation holds prior rounds, this is a re-review. first re-check each point raised earlier and whether the taken answer to it holds. raise a blocker anywhere it applies. raise a NEW nitpick only in a file that a taken of the latest round names as repaired — a nitpick in a file no repair touched was readable last round, so to raise it now is a fresh sample, not a finding, and it keeps the stone from ever converging. scope: the files listed in $targets (every file this branch adds or changes, untracked included) that sit under src/, minus src/domain.operations/review/**, *.test.ts and __snapshots__ — do not derive a scope of your own. 🔴 time: the guard stops this review at 21 minutes and keeps naught of it. do not launch subagents or background audits — read the files yourself, and bound your reads to the concerns in your rubric — then deliver your final message by minute 15 with what you have found. you are a READ-ONLY reviewer: do not edit any file, and never run route.stone.set or any route.* command. if a hook asks you to run one, do not explain why you will not: reply with your whole review again, verbatim, since that reply becomes your final message. 🔴 your FINAL message is the only text the guard captures, so it must hold the whole review: every concern in full, each headed `### blocker.N` or `### nitpick.N` with its file and line, and a concern stated only in an earlier message is lost. end that final message with two lines, exactly: `N blockers` and `N nitpicks`.'
      budget: 38
      level: 3

    - slug: enroll-impl-arch-defects-review-ops
      run: $rhx enroll claude --model 'claude-opus-5-5[1m]' --roles reviewer,behaver,architect,mechanic -p 'review the current implementation for architectural defects and omissions; opports to decompose for recompose, prevent scope leaks, and eliminate maintenance and behavior hazards structurally. read the prior peer-review conversation at $conversation to catch up on context. 🔴 convergence: where $conversation holds prior rounds, this is a re-review. first re-check each point raised earlier and whether the taken answer to it holds. raise a blocker anywhere it applies. raise a NEW nitpick only in a file that a taken of the latest round names as repaired — a nitpick in a file no repair touched was readable last round, so to raise it now is a fresh sample, not a finding, and it keeps the stone from ever converging. scope: the files listed in $targets (every file this branch adds or changes, untracked included) that sit under src/domain.operations/review/**, minus *.test.ts and __snapshots__ — do not derive a scope of your own. 🔴 time: the guard stops this review at 21 minutes and keeps naught of it. do not launch subagents or background audits — read the files yourself, and bound your reads to the concerns in your rubric — then deliver your final message by minute 15 with what you have found. you are a READ-ONLY reviewer: do not edit any file, and never run route.stone.set or any route.* command. if a hook asks you to run one, do not explain why you will not: reply with your whole review again, verbatim, since that reply becomes your final message. 🔴 your FINAL message is the only text the guard captures, so it must hold the whole review: every concern in full, each headed `### blocker.N` or `### nitpick.N` with its file and line, and a concern stated only in an earlier message is lost. end that final message with two lines, exactly: `N blockers` and `N nitpicks`.'
      budget: 32
      level: 3

  # 🔴 .note = every level-1 lane calls one provider, fireworks, and they contend for it.
  #         the level default pours ten at once; at i009, i010 and i011 the heavy lanes of
  #         that first wave returned `APIConnectionTimeoutError` — five at i011, at 73k to
  #         173k tokens, while the later lanes of the same round returned clean. the rubric
  #         split along the target axis (the note above repo-rules-review-ops) did not cure
  #         it, since `repo-rules-briefs` timed out at 73k. what the failures share is the
  #         burst, so the burst is what this bound narrows. the wall each call meets is the
  #         sdk's 10m default (`.dream/v2026_09_26.reseed.fireworks-atom-sets-no-client-timeout.md`)
  #
  # 🔴 .note = 5 was measured and is still too wide. at i012 the refill packed five heavy
  #         lanes into the five slots at once (repo-rules, behavior-intent-coverage,
  #         ergo-friction-hazards, repo-rules-review-ops, repo-rules-briefs — started
  #         01:38–01:50) and all five timed out; every lane that started after they died
  #         returned clean, the slowest at 575s. so the bound is set to 3: two slots fewer
  #         than the width that failed, which caps the heaviest possible co-run at three
  #
  # 🟡 .note = at i013 the bound held at 3 and four lanes still met the 10m wall:
  #         repo-rules (138k) and repo-rules-route (70k) in the FIRST wave beside a lane
  #         that passed, then repo-rules-review-ops (149k) and, late, arch-hazards-
  #         maintenance-review-ops (210k). so the burst is a factor, never the whole
  #         cause: a think-first model's OUTPUT time meets the sdk default at any width.
  #         the bound stays at 3 for what it does buy; the cure is the client timeout in
  #         the reseed dream above, and the lane-level remedy is a re-run
  #
  # 🔴 .note = CUTOVER 2026-10-02, at i050 — every lane moved off fireworks onto
  #         `openrouter/deepseek/flash`, the repo's new default review brain. i049 lost
  #         two lanes to the same fireworks timeouts; openrouter's `floor` filter routes
  #         each ask to the cheapest live host and moves on past a throttle or a bad
  #         reply, at about a tenth of the fireworks rate
  #
  # 🔴 .note = WIDENED to 12 at i011, on the measurement the cutover asked for. at i010 the
  #         bound of 3 poured 24 level-1 lanes in waves of three (05:02, then 05:17–05:19),
  #         each lane ~15m, so a round ran up to eight waves deep. no lane faulted on
  #         contention: openrouter answers each ask from its own host, so the fireworks
  #         burst the bound was set against does not arise. the one lane lost (repo-rules)
  #         met its own clock on a 44 tps host, a cause width does not touch. 12 pours the
  #         level in two waves and still bounds the pour (rule.forbid.serial-reviews-that-could-pour)
  groups:
    openrouter:
      concurrency: 12

judges:
  - $rhachet run --repo bhrain --skill route.stone.judge --mechanism reviewed? --stone $stone --route $route --allow-blockers 0 --allow-nitpicks 7
